  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `src/execution_coverage/progress.rs#mod[tests]/impl[RecordingTerm/indicatif::TermLike]/fn[move_cursor_right]` is appropriate.\n\n- **Permitted form:** The item is an explicit function with a body, and the citation is a fenced `{cite}` with substantive commentary in the item's own input.\n- **Unit-test-only:** The enclosing `mod tests` is marked `#[cfg(test)]`, so `RecordingTerm` is a unit-test-only adapter.\n- **No-op:** The function body is `Ok(())`. The adapter has to implement it only because the `indicatif::TermLike` trait requires it. It takes part in no application behavior.\n- **Indicatif never calls it:** The commentary says indicatif's single-line renderer never calls this callback. I could not read indicatif's source in this project, so I did not check that claim. It matches my understanding of how indicatif draws, and nothing in the project contradicts it. The progress tests in `progress.rs` use only fixed-width single-line displays through `ProgressDrawTarget::term_like`.\n- **PTY test:** The commentary says the real terminal implementation is exercised by a PTY integration test. That test exists in `tests/authoring_batch.rs#impl[Fixture]/fn[run_in_pty_with_args]`. It opens a PTY and runs the real binary, and `Progress::new` uses `console::Term::buffered_stderr()` as the draw target.\n\nThe explanation is substantive: it says why the callback runs no application logic, not merely that covering it is hard.",
